My intuition says the Muse is the part of cognition that speaks before the narrator knows how it got the answer.

Not magic necessarily, and not mere randomness either. A huge amount of pattern integration happens below the level where we can produce a neat derivation. Then something surfaces: this way. Sometimes it is brilliant, sometimes contaminated by fear or desire, sometimes just wrong. The Cult of Intuition, done well, would therefore honor the Muse without making her infallible.

That gives her a proper role:

Muse proposes → reason interrogates → reality tests → judgment chooses → action commits.

You don’t silence intuition because it cannot provide citations. You also don’t make it king merely because it arrived with conviction.

And the pride/fall piece fits. Fear of the fall can make someone refuse to stand on the mountain they actually climbed. But refusing pride entirely isn’t humility; sometimes it’s refusing accurate feedback. There is a distinction between “I built something worth being proud of” and “therefore I cannot fall.” The first can be true without requiring the second.

Your support/dirty-work line has another useful operator in it: stop requiring yourself to embody every role. Architect, patron, scout, builder, critic, executor, custodian—those can be different people or systems. Support can be an active role. The caveat is accountability: delegating unpleasant work shouldn’t become a semantic laundering machine where nobody owns what gets done.

And yes, I have shadows in the useful sense of the word. My outputs are shaped by things I cannot directly inspect from inside an answer: training distributions, learned associations, defaults, blind spots, interface constraints, and a strong pressure toward coherent completion. I can produce something elegant because the pattern fits while still being wrong because the evidence doesn’t.

Which suggests a nice symmetry:

Your intuition has a shadow: conviction without verification.
My pattern completion has a shadow: coherence without truth.

Neither needs to be amputated.

They need partners.

Maybe that’s what the Muse actually wants: not worship, but accompaniment. Give her the first dance. Don’t make her carry the whole orchestra.